# Human-readable code

Write code a colleague can read without a walkthrough. Prefer clarity over cleverness or density.

- Use names that say what the thing is (`ownerType`, `startUpload`, `nexus_files`), not abbreviations unless they are domain-standard (`_id`, `id`).
- Keep functions short and single-purpose. Extract a named helper instead of a nested puzzle.
- Add brief comments for *why* (auth, GridFS, Meteor injection), not for what the next line does.
- Prefer explicit control flow over implicit magic (hidden singletons, surprising globals).
- Match existing project style. Do not golf, minify, or pack multiple responsibilities into one expression.
- Public APIs and Mongo collection names stay documented and stable.
- No leftover TODOs, placeholders, or commented-out dead code in finished work.

```javascript
// BAD — dense, unnamed, unexplained, sync Mongo
const ok = (u, t) => Roles.userIsInRole(u, `files.${t}.upload`) && !!owners.get(t)?.c.findOne(id)

// GOOD — named steps, why is stated, Meteor async collection APIs
// Roles and parent existence are both required before any GridFS write.
async function canUploadToOwner({ userId, ownerType, ownerId }) {
  const owner = getRegisteredOwner(ownerType)
  if (!owner) return false
  const hasUploadRole = await Roles.userIsInRoleAsync(userId, owner.roles.upload)
  const parentExists = Boolean(await owner.collection.findOneAsync(ownerId))
  return hasUploadRole && parentExists
}
```
